      "title": "Protected Areas in Iran: Habitats, Designations and Conservation",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s protected landscapes, the distinction between national and international designations, and the challenges of effective conservation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "what-protection-means",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What protection means",
        "content": "A protected area is more than a scenic destination or an undeveloped landscape. The International Union for Conservation of Nature, usually abbreviated to IUCN, defines it through a clearly identified geographical space, a commitment to conserving nature over the long term, and management backed by legal or other effective arrangements. Its management categories distinguish objectives: a strictly controlled reserve, a national park conserving ecological processes, and an area protecting a particular natural feature serve different purposes. These are international management concepts; they should not be treated as an automatic translation of every Iranian legal designation. [s1]\n\nFor readers exploring Iran, this distinction changes the questions worth asking. What habitats does a boundary encompass? Which activities are regulated? Who manages the site? What evidence shows that its ecological values are being maintained? A designation establishes a conservation commitment, while its implementation needs separate examination. UNESCO’s account of the Hyrcanian Forests illustrates why: it describes legal protection alongside continuing problems with enforcement. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "forest-example",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The Hyrcanian forests: protection across a living landscape",
        "content": "The Hyrcanian forests form a belt of broad-leaved woodland along the southern and southwestern Caspian region. The World Heritage property protects selected components of this larger ecological landscape, rather than every remaining forest. UNESCO describes their importance in terms of ancient forest history, ecological processes, and plant species whose distributions reflect long periods of isolation. [s2]\n\nWithin Iran, the property involves forest, environment, and cultural-heritage authorities. That shared responsibility makes coordination consequential: a coherent conservation objective must carry across separate components and administrative responsibilities. UNESCO identifies seasonal grazing and wood collection as continuing pressures in some components and says that enforcement of access and use restrictions needs strengthening. International recognition therefore supplies neither proof of uniformly effective protection nor a guarantee that pressures have disappeared. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "wetlands-and-livelihoods",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Wetlands connect conservation with water use",
        "content": "Wetland conservation demonstrates how management extends beyond a site's apparent edge. In its account of the Conservation of Iranian Wetlands Project, the United Nations Development Programme describes work linking ecosystem management with agriculture, water efficiency, and local livelihoods. Lake Urmia was an initial pilot, followed by work involving Bakhtegan, Shadegan, and Anzali. [s3]\n\nThe programme’s approach matters because protecting wetland biodiversity also requires attention to pressures on the water resources that sustain it. Supporting farmers and diversifying incomes are described as ways to reduce those pressures. This is the implementing organization’s explanation of its activities and intended mechanisms; it is not an independent demonstration that the wetlands have recovered. Reported pilot results should likewise not be generalized into nationwide conservation outcomes. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-conservation-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading conservation claims carefully",
        "content": "For an international reader, the most useful interpretation combines ecological purpose, management responsibilities, and evidence of implementation. A forest property and a wetland programme reveal different conservation needs: regulating damaging uses within forest components, and addressing water use and livelihoods around wetlands. Neither can be understood from a designation alone. [s2][s3]\n\nThis page does not provide a national protected-area total or coverage percentage. Such figures require a dated inventory, consistent definitions, and treatment of overlapping boundaries. The sources inspected here support an explanation of conservation practice, but not a comparable annual account of Iran’s entire protected-area network.",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
