    "seo": {
      "title": "Natural Hazards in Iran: Earthquakes, Water and Disaster Risk",
      "description": "Explore how Iran’s earthquakes, floods, drought and subsidence interact with settlement, infrastructure, water use and vulnerability."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "hazard-and-disaster",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From physical events to disaster risk",
        "content": "Iran’s hazard geography combines active tectonics with water scarcity and episodes of destructive rainfall. The national post-disaster assessment of the spring floods documents a broad spectrum of hazards, including earthquakes, drought, floods, slope failures, heat, and sand and dust storms. These processes have different triggers, timescales, and geographical footprints; a single ranking cannot adequately describe them. [s1]\n\nA hazard is a potentially damaging process. Exposure concerns people, housing, infrastructure, and productive assets situated where it can occur. Vulnerability describes the conditions that make those exposed elements susceptible to harm, while capacity includes the resources available to manage risk. These distinctions explain why the same physical event can produce very different consequences in different communities. They also make disaster risk a question of development and preparedness, as well as geography. [s2]\n\nThis page introduces the relationships among the main branches. It does not assign provinces a single risk score or treat an earthquake magnitude, a flood loss, and a subsidence rate as interchangeable measurements.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "earthquakes-and-slopes",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Earthquakes and unstable ground",
        "content": "Western Iran provides a clear example of the connection between mountain building and seismic activity. The US Geological Survey explains the earthquake near the Iran–Iraq border in November 2017 through convergence between Arabia and Eurasia and faulting associated with the Zagros mountain belt. Earthquake risk therefore cannot be understood from weather or recent rainfall alone. [s3]\n\nSlope failures form another branch of the hazard landscape. Iran’s flood assessment identifies landslides and other forms of ground instability alongside its seismic exposure. For readers, the practical analytical distinction is between an initiating process and the additional damage it can set in motion: assessing shaking alone does not describe every possible consequence in unstable terrain. A local assessment needs evidence about both the hazard and the people or infrastructure exposed to it. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "water-extremes",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Drought and flooding belong in the same picture",
        "content": "An arid setting does not eliminate flood risk. The spring 2019 disaster followed successive waves of heavy rain, and the subsequent needs assessment examined damage and recovery in Golestan, Lorestan, and Khuzestan. It offers a concrete reminder that prolonged water scarcity and damaging rainfall can both shape Iran’s geography. Its detailed assessment covered selected provinces, so those findings should not automatically be read as a uniform national account. [s1]\n\nDrought develops differently from a sudden flood. UNDP’s country discussion links Iran’s low precipitation and high temperatures with water scarcity and identifies sand and dust storms as part of a wider regional environmental challenge. These descriptions establish relevant pressures, but they do not by themselves attribute an individual storm or drought episode to climate change. Event attribution and local forecasts require more specific evidence. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "subsidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Subsidence links water use to ground deformation",
        "content": "Land subsidence is the downward movement of the ground surface. In Iran, groundwater extraction is an important driver: removing water can compact aquifer sediments. A satellite-based study by Payne and colleagues describes widespread subsiding regions and finds that much of the rapid deformation examined is inelastic, meaning that replenishing water does not simply reverse the compaction. [s5]\n\nThis makes subsidence different from a temporary shortage of water. It can represent a lasting change in the aquifer and the land above it. The study also examines drought-related changes in the recoverable component of deformation, illustrating why human extraction and climatic conditions need to be considered together. Its satellite observation period is historical; the results should not be presented as a measurement of conditions everywhere in Iran at the editorial cutoff. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "risk-relationships",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "How hazards become potential losses",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  H[\"Potentially damaging physical process\"] -->|\"Hazard component\"| R[\"Disaster risk\"]\n  E[\"People and assets in affected places\"] -->|\"Exposure component\"| R\n  V[\"Susceptibility to harm\"] -->|\"Vulnerability component\"| R\n  C[\"Resources and ability to manage risk\"] -->|\"Capacity shapes potential consequences\"| R\n  R -->|\"Concerns possible future impacts\"| L[\"Loss of life, injury and damaged assets\"]\n```",
        "summary": "This conceptual diagram identifies the components of disaster risk. It is not a numerical model, a prediction, or a claim that every hazardous event causes a disaster.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpreting-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Read each measurement on its own terms",
        "content": "Hazard reports answer different questions. A tectonic explanation identifies a physical mechanism; a disaster assessment records impacts and recovery needs; a satellite study measures deformation during a defined observation period. Combining them is useful, but converting them into a common trend without compatible definitions would obscure their meaning. [s1][s3][s5]\n\nThe central lesson is that reducing losses involves more than recognizing hazardous places. Understanding exposure, vulnerability, and capacity helps connect physical geography with decisions about settlements, infrastructure, and preparedness. The branch pages examine the individual processes more closely. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s5"]
      }
    ],
