    "description": "Iran’s earthquake hazard reflects active deformation associated with the convergence of Arabia and Eurasia. The consequences of an earthquake depend on its rupture, local ground motion, and the vulnerability of buildings and infrastructure, rather than magnitude alone.",
    "tags": ["Earthquakes", "Tectonics", "Zagros", "Bam", "Seismic risk", "Ground shaking"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T12:31:28Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T12:31:28Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Earthquakes in Iran: Tectonics, Bam and Seismic Risk",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s active faults, what Bam and the 2017 western Iran earthquake reveal, and why magnitude alone does not explain damage."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "tectonic-setting",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A landscape under continuing deformation",
        "content": "Earthquakes occur when rock breaks and slips along a fault, releasing energy that travels as seismic waves. Iran’s western mountain belt offers a clear tectonic example: the US Geological Survey links the Zagros to convergence between the Arabian and Eurasian plates. Compression builds mountains while deformation is accommodated through faults capable of generating earthquakes. [s1][s2]\n\nThe earthquake of 12 November 2017 near the Iran–Iraq border had a moment magnitude of 7.3. Subsequent research identified rupture on a gently dipping fault beneath the northwestern Lurestan arc of the Zagros. This matters because an earthquake is not simply a point on a map. Its source occupies a fault surface, and the geometry and direction of rupture help determine how energy is released. The later study provides a more developed interpretation than an immediate preliminary bulletin. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "magnitude-and-shaking",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Magnitude does not describe every consequence",
        "content": "Magnitude expresses an earthquake’s size; the shaking experienced at a particular place depends on additional factors. Natural Resources Canada identifies distance from the source, focal depth, faulting, and the material beneath a site among the influences on ground motion. Buildings at different locations can therefore experience different shaking during the same earthquake. [s1]\n\nThis distinction is essential when reading Iranian disaster history. Comparing event magnitudes alone does not establish which settlement should have suffered more damage. Source characteristics must be considered alongside local conditions and the vulnerability of the built environment. A historical example can explain these mechanisms without being turned into a prediction for another city. [s1][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "bam",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Bam: a destructive earthquake close to a city",
        "content": "The earthquake that struck Bam in southeastern Iran on 26 December 2003 had a moment magnitude of 6.6. Manuel Berberian’s synthesis describes rupture on a north–south, right-lateral strike-slip fault: the blocks moved predominantly sideways relative to one another. The causative fault was not known before the earthquake, and little of its shallow slip appeared as surface rupture. [s4]\n\nBam demonstrates why a city's losses cannot be inferred from magnitude alone. The synthesis connects severe shaking with rupture propagation and directivity, the concentration of seismic energy toward the city. It also identifies poor infrastructure quality and inadequate risk management as contributors to the destruction. The interaction of the physical source with vulnerable construction is the central lesson; neither geology nor building condition alone tells the whole story. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "knowledge-and-limits",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What science can establish",
        "content": "Earthquake investigation can reconstruct fault motion, examine recorded shaking, and improve understanding of regional hazards. The study of the western Iran event combined seismic observations with satellite radar measurements, showing how different kinds of evidence constrain a rupture model. Such models explain a past event and inform hazard understanding; they do not specify the date of the next earthquake. [s3]\n\nThe US Geological Survey states that reliable prediction would require the time, location, and magnitude of a future event, and that scientists cannot currently provide this. Its emphasis is on reducing long-term consequences, including improving structural safety. For readers interpreting Iran’s seismic geography, the useful conclusion is that uncertainty about timing coexists with substantial knowledge about faults, shaking, and vulnerability. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s5"]
      }
    ],
